WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.158
Decision science is the study and practice of making better choices when information is incomplete,

00:00:05.158 --> 00:00:07.855
constraints are real, and tradeoffs cannot be avoided.

00:00:07.855 --> 00:00:09.878
It does not promise perfect answers.

00:00:09.878 --> 00:00:15.273
It gives people a way to improve the quality of their thinking before action is taken,

00:00:15.273 --> 00:00:19.656
while there is still time to clarify the question, compare options, examine assumptions,

00:00:19.656 --> 00:00:21.005
and reduce avoidable error.

00:00:21.005 --> 00:00:24.714
A useful starting point is to separate decisions, preferences, predictions, actions,

00:00:24.714 --> 00:00:25.388
and outcomes.

00:00:25.388 --> 00:00:28.760
A decision is a commitment to a course of action.

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:33.818
A preference is what someone would like to be true or would like to happen.

00:00:33.818 --> 00:00:36.515
A prediction is a judgment about the future.

00:00:36.515 --> 00:00:38.538
An action is what gets done.

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:41.910
An outcome is what happens after the action meets reality.

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:45.956
Those pieces influence each other, but they are not the same thing.

00:00:45.956 --> 00:00:49.665
This distinction matters because good decisions can still have bad outcomes.

00:00:49.665 --> 00:00:54.386
A security team may choose a well-reasoned patch plan, then experience an unrelated outage.

00:00:54.386 --> 00:00:57.083
A manager may make a sound staffing decision,

00:00:57.083 --> 00:01:00.118
then lose a key person to an unexpected opportunity.

00:01:00.118 --> 00:01:04.164
The bad result does not automatically prove the decision process was bad.

00:01:04.164 --> 00:01:07.536
Uncertainty, chance, timing, and external conditions can all affect outcomes.

00:01:07.536 --> 00:01:09.222
The reverse is also true.

00:01:09.222 --> 00:01:11.245
Bad decisions can sometimes get lucky.

00:01:11.245 --> 00:01:15.965
A team might skip analysis, ignore risk, and still avoid consequences for a while.

00:01:15.965 --> 00:01:18.663
If people judge only by the final result,

00:01:18.663 --> 00:01:21.360
they may reward luck and punish disciplined thinking.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:25.744
Decision science asks us to evaluate the process as well as the outcome,

00:01:25.744 --> 00:01:29.453
because repeated good process tends to produce better results over time.

00:01:29.453 --> 00:01:30.801
The discipline is practical.

00:01:30.801 --> 00:01:34.510
It applies to leadership, technical execution, security, operations, learning, product choices,

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:36.533
hiring, incident response, and personal planning.

00:01:36.533 --> 00:01:39.231
The goal is not to make uncertainty disappear.

00:01:39.231 --> 00:01:42.603
The goal is to make choices more explicit, more reviewable,

00:01:42.603 --> 00:01:45.300
and more likely to survive contact with reality.
